Next is the MOSFET selection. What we are looking for is a min 30V operating voltage because we will pass 24V voltage through it, and the amp value must be min 15A so it can work comfortably. Since we will use the MOSFET over GND, we will use an N-type MOSFET. The reason we choose THT is to be able to solder it onto the PCB. Also, we will use TO-220 as the package (casing). The options I have left are: IRLIZ44GPBF, STP36NF06L

IRLZ44N: Since this is the closest V value to 3.3V for Rds(on), I chose this MOSFET. Also, with a 4V ohm drain of 0.035, we will be able to provide lighting at values very close to 100% brightness.
So we chose our MOSFET and our ESP32. Next is our lux sensor. Since I don't have much knowledge here, I was torn between 2 sensors, but the sensor I chose is VEML7700 because it can go up to 140K lux. This is way above average, and it can work without getting blinded even at 12 PM noon in the desert. Also, it does the calculations itself. For this reason, the sensor I chose is VEML7700. That's all for today. Today was spent researching components. Tomorrow I will do the necessary analyses.
